# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "A Series of Assignments".
id: series-of-assignments
title: A series of assignments
topic: Sequential composition
level: 1
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "A Series of Assignments"
tags: assignment, sequence, wp, intervals

task:
  The reference document gives this exercise as a C function that
  returns `res`. We write it in the language of the course: the result is
  the value of the variable $res$ at the end.

  Compute by hand the weakest precondition of the program and deduce that
  it is correct with respect to its contract.

pre: -10 <= x <= 0 && 0 <= y <= 5
post: -10 <= res <= 10

program:
  {P}
  {R3}
  y := y + 10;     // i1
  {R2}
  x := x - 5;      // i2
  {R1}
  res := x + y     // i3
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The last statement, `res := x + y`
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i3
calc:
  wp(res := x + y, Q)
  = (-10 <= res <= 10)[res <- x + y]
  = -10 <= x + y <= 10
set: R1 = -10 <= x + y <= 10

step: The second statement, `x := x - 5`
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
calc:
  wp(x := x - 5, R1)
  = (-10 <= x + y <= 10)[x <- x - 5]
  = -10 <= (x - 5) + y <= 10
  = -5 <= x + y <= 15          // add 5 to the three parts
set: R2 = -5 <= x + y <= 15

step: The first statement, `y := y + 10`
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
calc:
  wp(y := y + 10, R2)
  = (-5 <= x + y <= 15)[y <- y + 10]
  = -5 <= x + (y + 10) <= 15
  = -15 <= x + y <= 5          // subtract 10 from the three parts
set: R3 = -15 <= x + y <= 5

step: Compare with the precondition
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: -10 <= x <= 0 && 0 <= y <= 5 ==> -15 <= x + y <= 5
why:
  We add the bounds of $x$ and $y$. The smallest possible value of
  $x + y$ is $-10 + 0 = -10$ and the largest is $0 + 5 = 5$. So
  $-10 <= x + y <= 5$, which is inside the interval from $-15$ to $5$.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The three assignments shift the interval of $x + y$. Going backwards, we
  undo each shift: $res = x + y$ must be in $[-10, 10]$ at the end, so
  $x + y$ must be in $[-15, 5]$ at the start. The precondition gives
  $x + y$ in $[-10, 5]$.
